// mc_teko.tk -- the self-hosted teko compiler as ONE unit (D64.9 route A,
// docs/design/plano-ngen-entrega4.md §64(e)): the same five parts of the mc
// core the `mc build` glue names (`build/teko.mc`), the 31 `.tk` modules
// of the `teko` package, and this project's own `main()`/`user_init()` --
// compiled by the taught teko itself instead of by the stock `mc`.
//
// It is a `.tk` file, so `tk_source_claim` (teko_fwd.tk) claims it and teko's
// own vocabulary applies inside it; the `#include`s below reach sources that
// are NOT claimed (`<mc/host>`, `<mc/core_min>`, `core_teko.mc`, `user.mc`),
// which is what lets the core's own files keep `type`, `out` and `params` as
// parameter names while `.tk` reads them as teko words. Nothing else lives
// here: every line is an include, so this file has no identifier of its own
// to collide with a taught word.
//
// The order is the glue's own -- `<mc/host>` first (the platform), the core,
// then the parts and `main()` (`core_teko.mc`), then the modules (`teko.tk`
// pulls its 30 siblings), then the project's `user_init()` (`user.mc`), which
// calls `teko_init()` and therefore has to come after it (S3, D64.7).
//
// Built by `scripts/bootstrap.sh`, which is what proves the fixed point: teko0
// (from `mc build . --config teko.toml`) compiles this file into teko1, teko1
// compiles it into teko2, teko2 into teko3, and `teko2.o` has to equal `teko3.o`.
#include <mc/host>
#include <mc/core_min>
#include "core_teko.mc"
#include "teko.tk"
#include "user.mc"
